WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.900
Diagnostics and logging are essential in RTOS systems because field failures can be hard to reproduce.

2
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:15.480
Logs, fault records, counters, reset reasons, watchdog events, and telemetry can help teams understand what happened.

3
00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:22.060
The challenge is to capture useful evidence without exposing secrets or destabilizing real-time behavior.

4
00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.900
Logs should be bounded and intentional.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.000
Avoid recording credentials, keys, sensitive configuration, private user data, or excessive raw external input.

6
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:39.020
Also avoid logging patterns that can consume too much time, memory, or storage during stress.

7
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.560
A diagnostic path should not make a fault worse.

8
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:46.120
Fault handling should preserve predictable behavior.

9
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:55.660
When a task fails, an assertion triggers, a driver reports an error, or memory checks detect a problem, the system should follow a documented response.

10
00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:03.200
That may involve safe-state behavior, controlled reset, fault recording, or service notification depending on the product.

11
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:06.680
Watchdog resets need useful context.

12
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:10.380
A reset reason without surrounding state can be hard to act on.

13
00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:18.000
At the same time, collecting too much state can expose sensitive data or interfere with timing.

14
00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:25.380
Teams should decide which counters, task states, and error categories are safe and useful.

15
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:27.900
Field diagnostics should be controlled.

16
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:37.760
Service modes, debug commands, trace settings, and diagnostic channels can be useful for maintenance, but production access should follow documented policy.

17
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:44.020
Controls should match the device environment, privacy expectations, and operational responsibilities.

18
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.540
Operational readiness means teams know how to interpret device evidence.

19
00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:55.280
Runbooks, dashboards, release notes, and fault taxonomies should connect embedded diagnostics to action.

20
00:01:55.380 --> 00:02:01.940
Good diagnostics make the device maintainable without turning every internal detail into field-visible data.
